=== tk purge ===
Delete a task record

Refuses while other tasks still block on it or name it as a parent: those
references would dangle, and a broken graph is worse than a stale record.

Usage: tk purge [FLAGS] <ID>

Arguments:
  <ID>  Task alias, ID, or ID prefix

Flags:
  -f, --force            Skip confirmation
      --scrub            Delete anyway, removing the references that point at it
      --if-rev <REV>     Reject the delete unless the task still has this
                         revision
  -h, --help             Print help

Global flags:
  -j, --json             Output as JSON
  -C, --dir <DIR>        Run in a different directory
      --tasks-dir <DIR>  Task store directory: exact path, must already exist
                         (no discovery)

=== tk recover ===
Drop a record's torn last line, left behind by an interrupted write

Only the incomplete final line is removed: it was never a complete event, so
nothing that was appended is lost.

Usage: tk recover [--dry-run] [ID]

Arguments:
  [ID]  Task alias, ID, or ID prefix (default: every record)

Flags:
      --dry-run          Report what would be dropped without changing anything
  -h, --help             Print help

Global flags:
  -j, --json             Output as JSON
  -C, --dir <DIR>        Run in a different directory
      --tasks-dir <DIR>  Task store directory: exact path, must already exist
                         (no discovery)

=== tk mv ===
Move a task to a different project

Identity is unaffected: a move changes one display field, and no reference
anywhere needs rewriting. To rename a project for every task in it, use `tk
config project rename`.

Usage: tk mv <SOURCE> <PROJECT>

Arguments:
  <SOURCE>   Task alias, ID, or ID prefix
  <PROJECT>  Target project name

Flags:
  -h, --help             Print help

Global flags:
  -j, --json             Output as JSON
  -C, --dir <DIR>        Run in a different directory
      --tasks-dir <DIR>  Task store directory: exact path, must already exist
                         (no discovery)

=== tk apply ===
Apply a batch of intents from stdin under one lock

Every intent is resolved and validated before anything is written, so a batch
that is wrong is rejected whole. Intents run in order; see `tk apply --help` in
the README for the accepted `op` values.

Usage: tk apply [--dry-run]

Flags:
      --dry-run          Validate the batch and report what would change,
                         without writing
  -h, --help             Print help

Global flags:
  -j, --json             Output as JSON
  -C, --dir <DIR>        Run in a different directory
      --tasks-dir <DIR>  Task store directory: exact path, must already exist
                         (no discovery)

=== tk clean ===
Remove old completed tasks (archives by default)

Usage: tk clean [--older-than <OLDER_THAN>] [--purge]

Flags:
      --older-than <OLDER_THAN>  Age in days (default: the store's clean-after
                                 setting)
      --purge                    Delete the records instead of archiving them
                                 (scrubs references)
  -h, --help                     Print help

Global flags:
  -j, --json                     Output as JSON
  -C, --dir <DIR>                Run in a different directory
      --tasks-dir <DIR>          Task store directory: exact path, must already
                                 exist (no discovery)

=== tk check ===
Check store integrity

Exits non-zero when anything is reported, so scripts and agents cannot mistake
findings for success.

Usage: tk check

Flags:
  -h, --help             Print help

Global flags:
  -j, --json             Output as JSON
  -C, --dir <DIR>        Run in a different directory
      --tasks-dir <DIR>  Task store directory: exact path, must already exist
                         (no discovery)

