Print the resolved task store location

Usage: tk path

Flags:
  -h, --help             Print help

Global flags:
  -j, --json             Output as JSON
  -C, --dir <DIR>        Run in a different directory
      --tasks-dir <DIR>  Task store directory: exact path, must already exist
                         (no discovery)

=== tk lock ===
Run a command while holding the store mutation lock

The lock serializes cooperating tk writers (and, when used for sync, the
checkout itself). The command must not run tk against the same store: the lock
is advisory and not reentrant.

Usage: tk lock [--scan <DIR>] [-- COMMAND]…

Arguments:
  [-- COMMAND]…  Command to run while holding the lock, after --

Flags:
      --scan <DIR>       Directory tree to scan for task stores to lock
                         (repeatable)
  -h, --help             Print help

Global flags:
  -j, --json             Output as JSON
  -C, --dir <DIR>        Run in a different directory
      --tasks-dir <DIR>  Task store directory: exact path, must already exist
                         (no discovery)

=== tk config ===
Show or set configuration

Usage: tk config <SUBCOMMAND>

Commands:
  alias    Manage directory aliases for -C
  project  Rename a project in every task that uses it
  set      Set one setting: project, priority, labels, or clean-after
  help     Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Flags:
  -h, --help             Print help

Global flags:
  -j, --json             Output as JSON
  -C, --dir <DIR>        Run in a different directory
      --tasks-dir <DIR>  Task store directory: exact path, must already exist
                         (no discovery)

